  "dom_id": "step-q146378",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "A children's soccer academy has $60$ children enrolled. From this population, $35%$ are left-footed. Let the random variable ${X}_{i}$ be equal to\n\n$$\n{X}_{i}=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\,i\\text{th member of the population is left-footed} \\\\ 0,\\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor $i=1,…,60.$ Find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0).$",
  "worked_solution": "We have a population of size $N=60,$ and $p=0.35$ represents the proportion of the population of left-footed children.\n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of  left-footed children equals\n\n$$\nNp=60(0.35)=21,\n$$\n\n- the number of right-footed children equals\n\n$$\nN(1-p)=60(1-0.35)=39.\n$$\n\nWe select two children of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0),$ the probability that the second child is left-footed given that the first child is right-footed.\n\nIf the first child is right-footed, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=60-1=59$ children remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np=21$ children remaining who are left-footed.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=0)=\\frac{Np}{N-1}=\\frac{21}{59}.\n$$",
